  description="A field-ready guide to the construction daily report — what belongs in each section, how to fill it out completely, and a template subcontractors can use today."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20">
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="mb-12">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3">Blog</p>
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight leading-tight">
          Construction Daily Report: What to Include (+ Template)
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-400">Published July 31, 2026 · 7 min read</p>
      </div>

      <div
        class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-brand-600"
      >
        <p>
          Many general contractors require subcontractors to submit a daily report form before they
          sign off on the day's work. But even when no one is asking for it, a complete daily report
          is one of the cheapest forms of protection a sub can carry. When a delay claim hits, when
          a GC disputes your change order, or when an inspector questions what was on site last
          Tuesday — your daily reports are the contemporaneous record that answers the question.
        </p>
        <p>
          This guide breaks down exactly what belongs in each section of a construction daily
          report, with notes on how to fill each one out so the record is actually useful. At the
          end you'll also find how to file these digitally so they're searchable and ready when you
          need them.
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-100 p-6 not-prose my-8">
          <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-3">Key takeaways</h2>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600 list-disc pl-5">
            <li>
              A daily report has eight core sections: identification, personnel, work performed,
              equipment, materials, visitors/inspections, safety, and delays.
            </li>
            <li>
              Specificity is what makes a report useful — location references, times, and quantities
              matter far more than paragraph summaries.
            </li>
            <li>
              Delays and disruptions caused by others are the most skipped section and the most
              valuable one when a claim arises.
            </li>
            <li>
              Contemporaneous daily records carry far more weight in disputes than recollections or
              after-the-fact reconstructions.
            </li>
            <li>
              Keep your own record even when the GC requires their form — the two serve different
              purposes.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="not-prose rounded-2xl border border-brand-100 bg-brand-50 p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Download the template</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
            The printable daily report this guide describes — crew table, weather, work performed,
            deliveries, delays, and safety sections — as an editable Word doc. No email required.
          </p>
          <a
            href="/templates-files/daily-report-template.docx"
            download
            data-template="daily-report"
            class="template-download mt-4 inline-flex items-center justify-center px-6 py-2.5 rounded-xl bg-brand-600 hover:bg-brand-700 text-white font-semibold text-sm transition-colors"
          >
            Download the free template (.docx)
          </a>
        </div>

        <h2>What is a construction daily report?</h2>
        <p>
          A construction daily report is a dated record of everything that happened on a job site on
          a given day. General contractors call it a daily report; subcontractors and field software
          often call it a site log or daily log. The content is the same: who was on site, what was
          built, what arrived, who visited, and what went wrong or was delayed.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most GCs have their own daily report forms. Many also have their own project-management
          portals where subs submit them. Fill out whatever the GC requires — and keep your own
          record too. The GC form manages the project; your record protects you.
        </p>

        <h2>The 8 sections every daily report should have</h2>

        <h3>1. Project identification and date</h3>
        <p>
          The header ties the report to a specific project and day. At minimum: project name,
          project number or address, date, and the name of the person filing the report. If you have
          a contract number or GC-assigned document number, include it. This sounds obvious but
          matters in disputes — a stack of reports with no header is nearly useless.
        </p>

        <h3>2. Personnel on site</h3>
        <p>
          List everyone your company had on the job that day: name or classification (foreman,
          journeyman, apprentice, laborer), hours worked, and what area or task they were assigned
          to. This section:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Supports labor billing on time-and-material work and <a href="/features/change-orders/"
              >change orders</a
            > — you can tie hours to scope
          </li>
          <li>Demonstrates adequate staffing if a delay claim arises against your schedule</li>
          <li>Gives you a record of actual vs. planned manpower you can review later</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Don't just write a headcount. "4 workers on site" is much weaker than "Foreman J. Reyes
          (8h, rough-in Building A), 2 journeymen (8h ea., panels 2A and 2B), 1 apprentice (8h,
          conduit pull 1st floor)."
        </p>

        <h3>3. Work performed</h3>
        <p>
          Describe what your crew actually completed that day. Use location references that match
          the drawings — building, floor, room number, gridline. "Installed 180 LF of 2-inch EMT
          conduit, Building A, 3rd floor, Rooms 301–312" is usable evidence. "Continued conduit
          work" is not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Also document work that was planned but could not be performed, and why. "Unable to start
          trim-out in Suite 205 — suspended ceiling grid not installed by others" is a
          contemporaneous record of a delay you did not cause. That entry, dated and filed the day
          it happened, is far more credible than raising it for the first time at closeout.
        </p>

        <h3>4. Equipment on site</h3>
        <p>
          List major equipment — boom lifts, excavators, welding machines, compressors, generators.
          Note rental equipment separately with start and end dates. If equipment sat idle due to
          access issues, weather, or delays caused by other trades, record that explicitly. Idle
          equipment time is a recoverable cost in many delay and disruption claims — but only if you
          documented it at the time.
        </p>

        <h3>5. Materials received and stored</h3>
        <p>
          Log all deliveries with quantities, vendor, and where materials were stored on site. Note
          any damaged or short shipments and how you handled them. This section serves two purposes:
          it creates a chain of custody for materials, and it supports
          <a href="/blog/aia-pay-application-guide/">stored materials billing</a> on your pay applications
          — the GC or owner needs to see that material was delivered and is on site before they'll pay
          for it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Also note long-lead items that were expected and did not arrive if that affected your
          schedule.
        </p>

        <h3>6. Visitors and inspections</h3>
        <p>
          Record every visitor to the site: owner's representatives, GC superintendents, architects,
          engineers, city inspectors. For inspections, note the result — passed, failed, partial —
          and what was inspected. If an inspector flagged an issue, document the specific item and
          the required corrective action. If the issue was caused by another trade's work or a
          design conflict, say that.
        </p>
        <p>
          An inspection that passes without documentation is still just your word. An inspection
          that fails without documentation is a liability.
        </p>

        <h3>7. Safety observations</h3>
        <p>
          Document safety toolbox meetings: date, topic, and attendance. Note any incidents,
          near-misses, or hazards — even those involving other trades. If you reported an unsafe
          condition to the GC or owner, record that you did and when. Safety documentation is both a
          legal requirement on many projects and a liability shield when incidents are investigated.
        </p>

        <h3>8. Delays, disruptions, and issues</h3>
        <p>
          This is the most important section and the one most subs skip entirely. Any time your work
          is delayed or disrupted by something outside your control, you need a real-time record:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Areas not accessible or not ready for your trade</li>
          <li>
            Missing or late information — RFI responses, shop drawing approvals, design
            clarifications
          </li>
          <li>Other trades blocking your work area</li>
          <li>Late or incorrect material deliveries caused by others' scope changes</li>
          <li>Schedule changes communicated by the GC</li>
          <li>
            Weather conditions that stopped or slowed work (note times and impact, not just "rain")
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The reason to document this in real time — not at the end of the month, not when you're
          preparing a change order — is credibility. A delay documented the day it happened is
          contemporaneous evidence. A delay summarized weeks later is a reconstruction, and
          reconstructions lose to contemporaneous records in almost every dispute.
        </p>
      </div>

