              Delays, disruptions, and issues
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">
              What was delayed or disrupted · Cause · Who is responsible · Impact on schedule or
              cost · Weather conditions and effect on work (include times)
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-brand-600"
      >
        <h2>What makes a daily report legally defensible</h2>
        <p>
          Filling out a report is not the same as filling it out well. A few practices that separate
          useful records from ones that get dismissed:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Specificity over summaries.</strong> "Rain from 7 AM to 10:30 AM, standing water in
            the excavation, underground rough-in stopped for 3.5 hours" is evidence. "Weather delay" is
            not.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>File it the same day.</strong> Contemporaneous means you wrote it while memory was
            fresh. If you're filling in reports on Friday for Monday through Thursday, you've already
            degraded their evidentiary value.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Be consistent.</strong> A log kept every single working day is far harder to challenge
            than one with gaps. Gaps invite the question of what happened on the missing days.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Attach photos.</strong> A photo with an embedded timestamp of the flooded excavation,
            the blocked work area, or the delivered material is stronger than words alone.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Don't editorialize.</strong> "Concrete pour delayed 4 hours because GC did not have
            pump truck on site as scheduled" is a factual statement. "GC completely mismanaged the concrete
            pour" is an opinion that will be attacked in any proceeding.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Who writes it, and when</h2>
        <p>
          Both questions have the same answer: the person who was on site, that same day. A field
          record written by someone who wasn't there — or written the following week from memory —
          keeps the format but loses most of what makes it worth keeping.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is a staffing question as much as a paperwork one. In BuildWorkPro, company admins,
          managers, members, and field crew can all create site logs, and the
          <a href="/features/site-logs/">Field Crew role</a> exists for exactly this: an on-site worker
          can write the day's record without getting access to bids, pricing, or billing. Make the log
          part of the end-of-day routine for whoever closes out the site, and it gets written by the person
          who actually watched the day happen.
        </p>
        <p>
          Once it's written, a manager can mark the entry reviewed — a second set of eyes, and a
          filterable status so nothing sits unchecked. That review step is what turns a pile of
          individual notes into a record the company stands behind.
        </p>

        <h2>What to leave out</h2>
        <p>
          Two things belong nowhere near a daily report, and both are easy to get wrong under
          pressure.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Arguments about who is at fault.</strong> Beyond the editorializing point above, this
          matters most when a directive arrives verbally. Record that a verbal directive was given, who
          gave it, and what it covered — then follow up in writing through the proper channel. The log
          is a factual record of what happened; the case for who owes whom belongs in a formal notice,
          where it carries weight and gets a response.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Blank days.</strong> A missing entry on a day when something went wrong is worse than
          a thin one. "Rained all day, no work performed — site flooded east of the utility trench" is
          a complete record of a day nothing got built. An empty day says either nothing happened or nobody
          was tracking, and you don't get to choose which one a reader assumes months later.
        </p>

        <h2>Using site logs in BuildWorkPro</h2>
        <p>
          In BuildWorkPro, <a href="/features/site-logs/">site logs</a> are a dedicated field module —
          not an afterthought tacked onto project notes. Each log is tied to a specific project and date.
          You add personnel on site, write your notes, and tag the entry by type: Progress, Issue, Delay,
          Safety, Delivery, or Inspection.
        </p>
        <p>
          The detail page lets you fill in Work Performed, Materials Received, Visitors on Site, and
          Issues as distinct fields — the same sections this template covers. Attach photos directly
          to the log entry. A manager can mark each log reviewed once it's been checked.
        </p>
        <p>
          The tag is the part people skip, and it's the part that pays. Adding one costs two seconds
          on the day you write the entry. Six months later, when you need every day the work was
          held up to support a schedule extension request, it's the difference between a filter and
          an afternoon of reading:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Delay</strong> entries become the timeline behind an extension claim — every day the
            work was held up, and why.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Safety</strong> entries are the documented record if an incident is questioned later.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Inspection</strong> entries capture what was inspected, by whom, and the result.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Issue</strong> entries are your running log of problems — which answers a GC who says
            a problem was never raised.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          You can filter the timeline or table view by project, tag, or reviewed status, and search
          by title or notes. That's what keeps a year of daily entries navigable instead of turning
          it into an undifferentiated wall of text.
        </p>
        <p>
          When a delay or disruption rises to the level of a scope change, you can create a
          <a href="/features/change-orders/">change order</a> directly from the log — the field record
          becomes the paper trail behind the cost. For teams also using
          <a href="/features/time-tracking/">time tracking</a>, crew hours tie to the same project
          and date, so labor and field activity are always in sync. See the
          <a href="/docs/field/site-logs/">site logs documentation</a> for a full walkthrough.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you want the broader picture on daily documentation habits and making logging a
          consistent crew routine, our
          <a href="/blog/construction-site-log-best-practices/"
            >construction site log best practices</a
          >
          guide covers the discipline side — what to do when, who should own it, and why paper logs fall
          short.
        </p>
      </div>

